    "description": {
      "type": "doc",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "text": "Maui is the second-largest Hawaiian island, built by two volcanoes joined by a low central valley. Haleakalā rises to 3,055 metres in the east, while the older West Maui Mountains catch the rain in the west.",
              "type": "text"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "text": "Polynesian voyagers settled the island centuries before Europeans arrived. Lahaina served as the royal capital of the Hawaiian Kingdom in the early 1800s and later as a whaling port, until a wildfire destroyed much of the town in August 2023. Shave ice and plate lunches of kalua pork and rice sell from roadside stands.",
              "type": "text"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "text": "Humpback whales arrive from December to April, with the most sightings from January to March. April, May, September and October bring fewer visitors. Book the Haleakalā sunrise reservation early, as slots open 60 days ahead and go fast.",
              "type": "text"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
